  "preamble": "Raising of the open-mid front vowel [ɛ] to close-mid [e], often as one step in a chain shift or as a merger of the two front mid vowels. In contemporary South Korean the vowels ㅐ /ɛ/ and ㅔ /e/ have merged as [e̞].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Raising, Merger",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Korean (Seoul)",
      "languageFamily": "Koreanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "ㅐ /ɛ/, e.g. 내가 /nɛ-ɡa/ 'I (subject)'",
          "to": "[e̞], 내가 [ne̞ɡɐ]",
          "note": "In South Korean speech the /ɛ/–/e/ contrast is lost and both are most commonly realized as [e̞]; some older speakers keep the distinction."
        },
        {
          "from": "개 /kɛ/ 'dog'",
          "to": "[kɛ] ~ [ke̞]"
        }
      ]
    },
